explain SendGrid/Twilio/Slack

Here's an explanation of each of these third-party services and how they fit into our platform:
SendGrid — Email delivery

SendGrid is a transactional email API used by notification-service to send emails (e.g., account confirmations, password resets, receipts, alerts — whatever the notification triggers are for our platform). Rather than running our own SMTP infrastructure, the service sends templated messages via SendGrid's API, and SendGrid handles delivery, bounce/spam handling, and deliverability at scale. It's configured via SENDGRID_API_KEY, which authenticates our service's requests to their API.
Twilio — SMS/voice messaging

Twilio is a communications API — in our case used for sending SMS notifications (e.g., 2FA codes, alerts, order/delivery updates depending on the product). It's configured via TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID (which account you're using) and TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN (which authenticates requests). Twilio bills per message/call, which is why for local dev you'd use a trial account and their test credentials rather than burning real usage.
Slack — Internal notifications / webhooks

Slack here isn't customer-facing — it's used for internal notifications. SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL is an Incoming Webhook URL that lets the service post messages directly into a Slack channel without needing a full bot/OAuth integration. This is likely how things like deployment notifications (#eng-deployments), on-call handoffs, or alert-related messages (#eng-oncall) get posted automatically by services rather than requiring someone to paste updates manually.
How they relate architecturally

All three are outbound integrations owned by notification-service, which is likely the single place in the platform responsible for external communication — probably consuming events off RabbitMQ (e.g., "user signed up," "payment failed," "deployment succeeded") and fanning them out to the appropriate channel (email via SendGrid, SMS via Twilio, or Slack for internal ops visibility).

