Summarize the environment config reference document.

Here's a summary of the Environment Configuration reference:
Overview

    All service configuration is done via environment variables, keeping config separate from code.
    Local dev: .env files (each service has a .env.example template to copy from).
    Staging/Production: AWS Secrets Manager, injected into Kubernetes pods at startup.

Required Variables (all services)

Every service needs: DATABASE_URL, REDIS_URL, NXS_API_KEY (internal Nexus API key), LOG_LEVEL, RABBITMQ_URL, SENTRY_DSN, AWS_REGION.
Service-Specific Variables

    api-gateway: RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE, AUTH_SERVICE_URL, CORS_ORIGINS
    auth-service: JWT_SECRET, JWT_EXPIRY_MINUTES, REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRY_DAYS, AUTH0_DOMAIN, AUTH0_CLIENT_ID, AUTH0_CLIENT_SECRET
    payment-service: STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET, PAYMENT_SERVICE_URL
    notification-service: SENDGRID_API_KEY, TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID, TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN, SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL, NOTIFICATION_SERVICE_URL
    frontend: NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL, NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_KEY (the publishable key — safe to expose client-side)

Optional Variables

PORT (override default), DEBUG, ENABLE_CORS, MAX_DB_CONNECTIONS, MAX_DB_OVERFLOW — all have sensible defaults.
Environment Differences

A table contrasts how key variables differ across environments — e.g., LOG_LEVEL is debug locally, info in staging, warn in production; DEBUG is true locally and false elsewhere; CORS_ORIGINS points to localhost:3000 locally vs. the staging/production domains.
Adding a New Environment Variable

A 6-step process: add to .env.example → add to the app's config module (config.py/config.ts) → add the secret to AWS Secrets Manager for staging/prod → update Terraform → update the Kubernetes manifest → document it in this reference.
Loading Configuration in Code

Shows the standard pattern for each stack:

    Python (FastAPI): pydantic_settings.BaseSettings reading from .env
    TypeScript: dotenv.config() populating a plain config object

Security Notes

    Never commit .env files (they're git-ignored).
    Never log environment variable values.
    Use AWS Secrets Manager for all staging/production secrets.
    Points to the secrets management doc for rotation and access policy details.

Summarize the secrets management document.

Here's a summary of the Secrets Management document:
Golden Rule

Never hardcode secrets in source code or documentation. Any hardcoded secret found should be reported immediately in #eng-security and the credential rotated.
Where Secrets Live by Environment

| Environment | Storage | Access | |-------------|---------|--------| | Local | .env files | Developer's machine only | | Staging/Production | AWS Secrets Manager | Injected into pods at startup | | CI/CD | GitHub Repository Secrets | Injected as env vars |
Local Development

    Copy .env.example → .env, fill in values, never commit (.gitignore'd).
    Always use test/sandbox credentials locally: Stripe sk_test_ keys, SendGrid/Twilio sandbox creds, local Docker DB password, and a dev Nexus key from the shared team vault.

AWS Secrets Manager (Staging/Production)

    Secrets stored as key-value pairs, defined via Terraform, referenced in Kubernetes manifests, and injected as env vars at pod startup.
    Naming convention: platform/{environment}/{service}/{variable_name} (e.g. platform/production/payment-service/stripe_secret_key).
    Only team leads have direct access to view secrets; others must ask a team lead or check a staging pod's env via kubectl exec ... env | grep <VAR>.

Nexus API Keys

    Used for internal service-to-service auth, one key per service.
    Generated via the Nexus admin panel, stored in Secrets Manager, injected as NXS_API_KEY.
    Rotated quarterly (Jan 15 / Apr 15 / Jul 15 / Oct 15).

Rotation Procedures

    Standard (planned): generate new value → update Secrets Manager → rolling restart → verify health → revoke old value.
    Emergency (compromised): rotate immediately → rolling restart of affected services → revoke old secret → post in #eng-security → investigate → follow incident response process if warranted.

Adding a New Secret

Create it in AWS Secrets Manager → add to Terraform → update the Kubernetes manifest → update service code to read it → document it in the environment config reference.
What NOT to Do

Don't commit .env files, paste secrets in Slack (even DMs), store them in Jira/Confluence, log secret values, email them, or use production secrets locally.

