Quote the security practices document in full.

Here's a summary of everything in the security practices document, section by section:

Input Validation — All user input must be validated server-side (never trust client-side validation alone). Python services use Pydantic models (with EmailStr, Field length constraints, etc.) for FastAPI request validation; TypeScript uses Zod schemas validated in route handlers.

SQL Injection Prevention — Always use parameterized queries via the ORM (SQLAlchemy for Python, Prisma for TypeScript). Never concatenate strings into SQL queries or pass user input directly into raw SQL. If raw SQL is unavoidable, use parameter binding (e.g., text("... WHERE email = :email") with a params dict).

XSS Prevention — React (Next.js) auto-escapes rendered content by default; avoid dangerouslySetInnerHTML unless truly necessary. API responses shouldn't reflect user input in error messages without sanitization. CSP headers are set at the Nginx layer to restrict script sources.

CORS — Configured on the API Gateway via the CORS_ORIGINS variable: local allows http://localhost:3000, staging allows https://staging.internal, production allows https://app.internal. Wildcard (*) origins are disallowed in staging/production.

HTTPS — All external traffic uses TLS 1.3, terminated at Nginx. Internal service-to-service traffic inside the Kubernetes cluster is unencrypted since the cluster network is isolated. Certificates are managed via AWS Certificate Manager with auto-renewal.

Authentication and Authorization — JWTs are validated on every request via API Gateway middleware; JWT scopes enforce authorization. Service-to-service calls authenticate using Nexus API keys.

Dependency Management — Dependabot runs weekly scans on all repos. Critical vulnerabilities (CVSS 9+) must be patched within 24 hours; high vulnerabilities (CVSS 7–8.9) within a week. Dependabot PRs should be reviewed promptly.

Secrets — Never hardcode secrets in source code, never commit .env files, never log secrets/tokens/credentials. Staging and production secrets live in AWS Secrets Manager (cross-referenced to the secrets management policy).

